    "media:imagetext": "- 3 -\nThe Escolta is a row of dirty wooden shacks for the most part with\nthousands upon thousands of Filipinos milling around in a maze of jeeps,\ncarametas, trucks, motor cars and taxis.\nThe din, dirt and confusion is\nbeyond description. In short, the far East has changed and will never again\nreturn to the life out here as we once knew it.\nReturning to Hongkong Saturday morning, there was no PAA schedule\nfor several days so I came over with Philippine Airways in a very fine DC-4.\nThree other airplanes left that same morning all DC-3 type ships (not so good\nfor crossing so many miles of ocean) for China and I remembered that 16 years\nago we made the first round trip from Chine to the Philippines!\nReturning here I found a cable relayed from New York to phone Geerge\nSellett.\nThe 'phone compeny said they would put me on the weiting list and\nperhaps within four or five days I might get through.\nHowever, All1 knew the\nPresident and asked him for help. In a half hour I had George on the phone\nHe had planned to ask me to come out from New York\nand was of course very pleased to know that I was already here. I called\nagain the next day and got a much better connection.\nHe said the \"liberation\"\neuthorities were anxious to see us and were very glad that we were here.\nthought that we would be permitted to resume Pan American service within e\nfew days or maybe a week.\nThat was the 5th of June and todey on the 10th me\nhave received no further word.\nHe hed lunch with Lewis Clerk yesterday and he advised us very\nstrongly to go over to Macao - intimating that Macao might become a very im-\nportat point. You can draw your own conclusions! Bondy and I plan to go\nover there Sunday the 12th and spend a few days. Will probably try to get\nthe Government to complete an air strip which is already partially con-\nstructed.\nWill call on Gellion and our other friends over there.\nOf course\nif a green light is received from Shanghai will come right back.\nMy views regarding the whole Chinese situation have changed since\narriving here and talking to American, British and Chinese officials. I had\nhoped that it might be possible for the Nationelists to get together and hold\na \"beach-head\" somewhere in South China. Of course anything can happen as\nevidenced by the man who now sits in the White House.\nBut the situation\nappears absolutely hopeless. Chiang Kai-shek will not let go. He is still\nplaying one political leader against another so that none will emerge to\nchallenge his position.\nGeneral Li - the acting President and General Pai\nwere the former strong men of Kwaagsi, the model Province.\nThe Generslissimo\nis afraid that they may again gain complete control of Kwangsi and break away\nfrom the so-celled \"Central Government\", so he has ordered General Pal to make\na stend with his Kwangsi troops au leng\nYang south of Changsha on the Hankow-\nCanton Railroad. Chiang hopes - so they say - that General Pai and his troops\nwill be taken by the Communists and thus unable to retrest to Kwangsi. At\nleast that is the opinion of some usually well-informed\npeople. You remember\nthe old proverb - \"Brothers stop quarrelling when there's a tiger at the door.\"\nWell, the Nationalists are not following that idea and the concensus of\nopinion is that the Communists will take Canton just as soon as their troops\nhave time to walk that far.\nThere may be a few provinces which will hold out\nfor months or maybe years against the Communists but they are in the west and\nnorthwest beyond the reach of reil or river traffie and of little importance\n58",
